<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Juan Perón</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1895">1895</a>–<a href="/years/1974">1974</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Juan Domingo Perón</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Juan Perón was an Argentine army officer who rose to power as labour minister after the 1943 coup and was elected president in 1946. With his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eva-peron">Eva Perón</a> he built a mass movement based on trade unions, industrialisation and social welfare. Overthrown in 1955, he lived in exile until returning to the presidency in 1973, dying in office the next year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juan-peron.jpg" alt="Juan Perón" width="320" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Juan_Domingo_Per%C3%B3n_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Juan Domingo Perón was born in Lobos, Buenos Aires Province, on 8 October 1895, the son of Juana Sosa Toledo and Mario Tomás Perón. His father's family was originally Spanish but had settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, and his great-grandfather emigrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> in the 1830s.</p><p>Perón entered military college in 1911 and rose through the ranks. He supported the 1930 coup against President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hipolito-yrigoyen">Hipólito Yrigoyen</a>, a decision he later regretted. Between 1939 and 1941 he served as a military attaché in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a>'s Italy, where he developed many of his ideas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Perón took part in the 1943 revolution and became Minister of Labor, then Minister of War and Vice President, gaining a name for labour reforms. Political disputes forced him to resign in October 1945, and he was arrested. On 17 October workers gathered in the Plaza de Mayo to demand his release. He won the presidential election of 24 February 1946 by about 11% against an alliance of the Radical Civic Union and other parties, after he turned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> ambassador's accusation of fascist ties into a choice between Perón and Braden.</p><p>His first term brought industrialisation, expanded social rights, tuition-free universities and, in 1947, votes for women, a measure tied to the work of his wife Eva Duarte. The government nationalised the Central Bank. It also fired dissidents, controlled the press and dismissed over 2,000 university professors. Several Nazi war criminals found refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>. Perón was re-elected in 1951, but his second term began amid drought and a trade deficit. After conflict with the Catholic Church, pro-Church elements of the armed forces bombed the Plaza de Mayo in June 1955, killing about 300 civilians, and a coup deposed him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Perón lived in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a>, Venezuela, Panama and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> while the Peronist party was outlawed. When the Peronist Héctor Cámpora won the 1973 election, Perón returned on 20 June 1973 amid the Ezeiza massacre and was himself elected president in October. Violence between left- and right-wing Peronists continued, and he could not resolve it. He died on 1 July 1974 and his third wife, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabel-peron">Isabel Perón</a>, succeeded him as vice president.</p><p>Supporters praise the Peróns for efforts to reduce poverty and dignify labour; detractors consider them demagogues and dictators. The movement continues mainly as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justicialist-party">Justicialist Party</a>. Perón was buried in La Chacarita Cemetery; in 1987 his tomb was desecrated and his hands stolen, a crime that remains unresolved.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Juan Perón” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Great Depression ended the export model, and from the 1930s, above all under <b>Juan Perón</b> after 1946, governments promoted import-substituting industry, nationalised railways and utilities and expanded labour rights and welfare.</blockquote><a href="/empire/argentine-republic">Argentine Republic · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Juan Perón</b>, an army officer who had served as a military attaché between 1938 and 1940 and drew on his experiences in Europe, rose after the coup of 1943.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/peronism">Peronism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The &quot;Peronist March&quot;, officially &quot;Los muchachos peronistas&quot; (&quot;The Peronist Boys&quot;), is the anthem of the Peronist movement founded and led by <b>Juan Domingo Perón</b> and the official song of Argentina's Justicialist Party.</blockquote><a href="/songs/peronist-march">Peronist March</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Lobos, in what is now <a href="/countries/argentina">Argentina</a>. Died in Quinta presidencial de Olivos, in what is now <a href="/countries/argentina">Argentina</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-35.19,-59.1&amp;year=1946" aria-label="Lobos on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3054 3436 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Juan Perón"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3504 3706h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3504 3706h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-35.19,-59.1&amp;year=1946">What was here in 1946? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
